Article 10 The following facts need not be proved by the parties:
(1) Natural laws, theorems, and laws;
(2) Facts known to the general public;
(3) Facts presumed according to legal provisions;
(4) Other facts presumed based on known facts and rules of daily life experience;
(5) Facts confirmed by effective awards of arbitration institutions;
(6) Basic facts confirmed by legally effective judgments or rulings of the People's Court;
(7) Facts proved by effective notarized instruments.
Except for the facts in items (2) to (5) of the preceding paragraph, where the party has contrary evidence sufficient to rebut them; and except for the facts in items (6) and (7), where the party has contrary evidence sufficient to overturn them.

Article 14 Electronic data includes the following information and electronic documents:
(1) Information published on network platforms such as web pages, blogs, and microblogs;
(2) Communication information of network application services such as SMS, email, instant messaging, and communication groups;
(3) Information such as user registration information, identity authentication information, electronic transaction records, communication records, and login logs;
(4) Electronic documents such as documents, pictures, audio, video, digital certificates, and computer programs;
(5) Other information stored, processed, and transmitted in digital form that can prove the facts of a case.

Article 36 The People's Court shall examine whether the evaluation report issued by the expert evaluator contains the following contents:
(1) The name of the commissioning court;
(2) The content and requirements of the commissioned evaluation;
(3) The evaluation materials;
(4) The principles and methods on which the evaluation is based;
(5) A description of the evaluation process;
(6) The evaluation opinion;
(7) The letter of commitment.
The evaluation report shall be signed or sealed by the expert evaluator, and shall be accompanied by the corresponding qualification certificate of the expert evaluator. Where an institution is commissioned for evaluation, the evaluation report shall be sealed by the evaluation institution and signed by the personnel who conducted the evaluation.

Article 40 Where a party applies for re-evaluation, the People's Court shall permit it under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The expert evaluator does not have corresponding qualifications;
(2) The evaluation procedure seriously violates the law;
(3) The evaluation opinion is obviously insufficiently based;
(4) Other circumstances where the evaluation opinion cannot be used as evidence.
Where the circumstances in items (1) to (3) of the preceding paragraph exist, the evaluation fees already collected by the expert evaluator shall be returned. If the evaluator refuses to return them, the matter shall be handled pursuant to the provisions of the second paragraph of Article 81 of these Provisions.
Where defects in the evaluation opinion can be resolved by correction, supplementary evaluation, supplementary cross-examination, or re-cross-examination, the People's Court shall not permit an application for re-evaluation.
In the case of re-evaluation, the original evaluation opinion shall not be used as the basis for determining the facts of the case.

Article 47 Under the following circumstances, the party controlling documentary evidence shall submit the documentary evidence:
(1) Documentary evidence that the party controlling the documentary evidence has previously cited in the litigation;
(2) Documentary evidence made for the benefit of the other party;
(3) Documentary evidence that the other party has the right to consult and obtain according to law;
(4) Account books and original accounting vouchers;
(5) Other circumstances under which the People's Court considers that documentary evidence shall be submitted.
Where the documentary evidence listed in the preceding paragraph involves State secrets, trade secrets, privacy of a party or a third party, or circumstances where confidentiality is required by legal provisions, it shall not be openly cross-examined after submission.

Article 55 Under any of the following circumstances, the time limit for adducing evidence shall be determined in the following manner:
(1) Where a party raises an objection to jurisdiction in accordance with Article 127 of the Civil Procedure Law, the time limit for adducing evidence shall be suspended, and the calculation shall resume from the date on which the ruling rejecting the jurisdictional objection takes effect;
(2) Where a party is added, a third party with independent claims participates in the litigation, or a third party without independent claims participates in the litigation upon notice by the People's Court, the People's Court shall determine a time limit for adducing evidence for the party newly participating in the litigation in accordance with Article 51 of these Provisions, and such time limit shall apply to the other parties;
(3) For a case remanded for retrial, the people's court of first instance may, in light of the specific circumstances of the case and the reasons for the remand, determine at its discretion a time limit for adducing evidence;
(4) Where a party adds or changes its claims or files a counterclaim, the People's Court shall re-determine a time limit for adducing evidence based on the specific circumstances of the case;
(5) Where service is made by public announcement, the time limit for adducing evidence shall be calculated from the day after the expiration of the announcement period.

Article 61 When cross-examining documentary evidence, physical evidence, or audio-visual materials, the parties shall present the original or the original object of the evidence, except under any of the following circumstances:
(1) There is indeed difficulty in presenting the original or the original object, and the People's Court has permitted the presentation of a copy or reproduction;
(2) The original or the original object no longer exists, but there is evidence proving that the copy or reproduction is consistent with the original or the original object.

Article 62 Cross-examination generally shall be conducted in the following order:
(1) The plaintiff presents evidence, and the defendant and the third party cross-examine with the plaintiff;
(2) The defendant presents evidence, and the plaintiff and the third party cross-examine with the defendant;
(3) The third party presents evidence, and the plaintiff and the defendant cross-examine with the third party.
With respect to evidence investigated and collected by the People's Court upon the application of a party, after the judicial personnel explain the circumstances of the investigation and collection of evidence, the party that made the application shall cross-examine with the opposite party and the third party.
With respect to evidence investigated and collected by the People's Court ex officio, after the judicial personnel explain the circumstances of the investigation and collection of evidence, the opinions of the parties shall be heard.

Article 87. The trial personnel may examine and determine a single piece of evidence from the following aspects:
(1) Whether the evidence is an original document or original object, and whether the copies or reproductions are consistent with the original document or original object;
(2) Whether the evidence is relevant to the facts of the case;
(3) Whether the form and source of the evidence comply with legal provisions;
(4) Whether the content of the evidence is true;
(5) Whether the witness or the person providing the evidence has an interest relationship with the party.

Article 90. The following evidence shall not be used alone as the basis for determining the facts of a case:
(1) Statements of the parties;
(2) Testimony given by a person without or with limited capacity for civil conduct that is not commensurate with his or her age, intellectual condition, or mental health condition;
(3) Testimony given by a witness who has an interest relationship with a party or his or her agent;
(4) Audio-visual materials or electronic data with doubtful points;
(5) Copies or reproductions that cannot be checked against the original document or original object.

Article 93. The People's Court shall comprehensively determine the authenticity of electronic data by taking into consideration the following factors:
(1) Whether the hardware and software environments of the computer system relied upon for the generation, storage, and transmission of the electronic data are complete and reliable;
(2) Whether the hardware and software environments of the computer system relied upon for the generation, storage, and transmission of the electronic data are in a normal operating state, or, if not in a normal operating state, whether such state has any impact on the generation, storage, and transmission of the electronic data;
(3) Whether the hardware and software environments of the computer system relied upon for the generation, storage, and transmission of the electronic data have effective monitoring and verification means for preventing errors;
(4) Whether the electronic data has been completely stored, transmitted, and extracted, and whether the methods for storage, transmission, and extraction are reliable;
(5) Whether the electronic data was formed and stored in normal transactions;
(6) Whether the subject that stores, transmits, and extracts the electronic data is appropriate;
(7) Other factors affecting the integrity and reliability of the electronic data.
If the People's Court considers it necessary, it may examine and determine the authenticity of the electronic data through methods such as expert evaluation or inspection.

Article 94. The People's Court may confirm the authenticity of electronic data under any of the following circumstances, unless there is contrary evidence sufficient to rebut it:
(1) The electronic data was submitted or kept by a party and is unfavorable to that party;
(2) The electronic data was provided or confirmed by a neutral third-party platform that records and preserves electronic data;
(3) The electronic data was formed in normal business activities;
(4) The electronic data was kept in an archival management manner;
(5) The electronic data was stored, transmitted, and extracted in the manner agreed upon by the parties.
If the content of electronic data has been notarized by a notarization authority, the People's Court shall confirm its authenticity, unless there is contrary evidence sufficient to overturn it.
